  "payload": {
    "rawResponseText": "An owner looking to **preserve individuality** should consider **soft brands** and **collection brands**—affiliations that let the hotel keep its own name, design, and character while accessing the parent company’s distribution, loyalty, and marketing systems.[1][2][4][7][16]\n\nThe main options fall into two broad types:\n\n- **Corporate soft brands**: these are run by major hotel groups and are designed for independent hotels that want the backing of a large chain without full standardization.[4][7][9]\n- **Independent or referral collections**: these are collections of distinctive hotels that affiliate for sales, reservations, and marketing support while keeping strong local identity and operational flexibility.[2][7][12]\n\nWell-known examples mentioned in the results include affiliations connected to large chains such as **Marriott International** and **Choice Hotels International**, as well as representation groups like **Preferred Hotels & Resorts** and **The Leading Hotels of the World**.[7]\n\nFor an owner, the appeal is that these models typically provide:\n\n- **Brand distribution and reservations support** through global systems.[1][3][4]\n- **Access to loyalty programs** that can drive repeat business.[4][7][9]\n- **More creative and operational flexibility** than a traditional hard franchise.[2][3][4]\n- **Lower or more favorable fees and requirements** in many cases.[1][3][6]\n\nIf you want, I can also provide a **shortlist of major soft brands and collections by segment**—luxury, upscale, boutique, and upper-upscale.",
